🚀 Day 20 — Rendering Lists in React

Today we're learning one of the most important React concepts:

Rendering Lists using .map() and the key prop

This is something you'll use constantly in real React applications.

For example:

Product lists
User lists
Blog posts
Comments
Menu items
Orders
Notifications
Shopping carts
Job listings
1. What is List Rendering?

Suppose you have this JavaScript array:

const fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Mango"];

You want React to display:

Apple
Banana
Mango

Instead of manually writing:

<h2>Apple</h2>
<h2>Banana</h2>
<h2>Mango</h2>

we can tell React:

"Take every item from this array and create an element for it."

We normally do this using JavaScript's .map() method.

2. Why Do We Need List Rendering?

Imagine an e-commerce website has 10,000 products.

You obviously cannot write:

<h2>iPhone</h2>
<h2>Samsung</h2>
<h2>OnePlus</h2>
...

Instead, the products come from data:

Database
   ↓
Backend API
   ↓
React
   ↓
Array of products
   ↓
.map()
   ↓
Product components
   ↓
Screen

So list rendering allows React to turn data into UI.

3. Real-World Analogy

Imagine a teacher has a list of students:

Students:


Rahul
John
Priya
Anjali

The teacher says:

"For every student, create one ID card."

You don't manually create each ID card.

You use the same process repeatedly:

Student 1 → ID Card
Student 2 → ID Card
Student 3 → ID Card
Student 4 → ID Card

React does something similar:

Array item → React element
Array item → React element
Array item → React element

That's list rendering.

4. Syntax

The basic syntax is:

array.map((item) => {
  return <Element>{item}</Element>;
});

For example:

const fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Mango"];


function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      {fruits.map((fruit) => {
        return <h2>{fruit}</h2>;
      })}
    </div>
  );
}
