🔑 The key Prop
8. What is key?

When React renders a list, each item should have a unique key.

Example:

{items.map((item) => {
  return <p key={item}>{item}</p>;
})}

Now each item has a key:

Milk  → key="Milk"
Bread → key="Bread"
Eggs  → key="Eggs"
Why does React need a key?

Imagine React previously rendered:

Apple
Banana
Mango

Then the data changes:

Apple
Orange
Banana
Mango

React needs to efficiently determine:

"What changed?"

The keys help React identify individual list items.

Visual Explanation of Keys

Without keys:

Old:


Apple
Banana
Mango


       ↓ change


New:


Apple
Orange
Banana
Mango

React has to determine which elements changed.

With keys:

Old:


1 → Apple
2 → Banana
3 → Mango


       ↓


New:


1 → Apple
4 → Orange
2 → Banana
3 → Mango

React can better identify the items.

9. The Correct Modern Pattern
const fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Mango"];


function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      {fruits.map((fruit) => {
        return <h2 key={fruit}>{fruit}</h2>;
      })}
    </div>
  );
}


export default App;

The important part is:

key={fruit}
10. Important Rule About Keys

Keys should ideally be:

Unique and stable.

For example, if your API gives:

const products = [
  {
    id: 101,
    name: "iPhone"
  },
  {
    id: 102,
    name: "Samsung"
  },
  {
    id: 103,
    name: "OnePlus"
  }
];

Use:

key={product.id}

Not:

key={product.name}

if names might not be unique.

And generally avoid:

key={Math.random()}

because the key changes every render.

