17. Common Mistakes
❌ Mistake 1 — Forgetting key
{users.map((user) => (
  <p>{user.name}</p>
))}

Better:

{users.map((user) => (
  <p key={user.id}>{user.name}</p>
))}
❌ Mistake 2 — Using array index unnecessarily

You might see:

key={index}

This works technically:

{users.map((user, index) => (
  <p key={index}>{user.name}</p>
))}

But if the list can be reordered, inserted into, or deleted from, an index can cause confusing UI/state behavior.

Prefer:

key={user.id}

when a stable ID exists.

❌ Mistake 3 — Using random keys

Avoid:

key={Math.random()}

The key changes every render.

❌ Mistake 4 — Returning multiple elements incorrectly

This can be problematic:

users.map((user) => {
  return (
    <h2>{user.name}</h2>
    <p>{user.age}</p>
  );
});

A component must return one JSX root.

Use:

users.map((user) => {
  return (
    <div key={user.id}>
      <h2>{user.name}</h2>
      <p>{user.age}</p>
    </div>
  );
});

Or use a Fragment:

users.map((user) => (
  <React.Fragment key={user.id}>
    <h2>{user.name}</h2>
    <p>{user.age}</p>
  </React.Fragment>
))
18. Interview Questions
Q1. What is list rendering in React?

Answer:

List rendering means generating multiple React elements from an array of data, usually using JavaScript's .map() method.

Q2. Why do we use map()?

Answer:

Because map() lets us transform every array item into a React element.

Q3. Why does React need keys?

Answer:

Keys help React identify individual elements in a list so it can efficiently determine which items were added, removed, or changed.

Q4. What makes a good key?

Answer:

A good key should be:

Unique among sibling elements
Stable
Associated with the actual data

Usually an ID from the database is ideal.

Q5. Why shouldn't we use Math.random() as a key?

Answer:

Because it generates a new value on each render, causing React to treat items as different elements unnecessarily.

Q6. Is key available inside the child component as a normal prop?

No.

For example:

<Product key={product.id} />

You cannot expect:

function Product(props) {
  console.log(props.key);
}

key is a special React attribute.

If the component needs the ID, pass it separately:

<Product key={product.id} id={product.id} />
